  "description": "GAP-2 closure test (bean qou-wt60): tests the remaining Markov move — CONJUGATION β ↦ γβγ⁻¹ — for the Gröbner–Shirshov coefficient sum S(β)=Σ_w c_w(q), the move left untested when gb_writhe_normalised_readout_probe closed stabilisation (M2). Markov's theorem needs both, so this decides whether the writhe-normalised readout Ŝ=S·(1−h)^{(e−n)/2} is a genuine knot invariant. Conjugation preserves BOTH the exponent sum (e is a homomorphism B_n→ℤ; the γ,γ⁻¹ contributions cancel) and the strand count, so the correction factor is literally unchanged and Ŝ is conjugation-invariant iff the raw S is — the normalisation is orthogonal to this move and can neither rescue nor damage the answer. The question is genuinely open a priori: S is the all-ones functional T_w↦1, which is NOT the Markov trace and not an algebra map (it would force h=0 on the Hecke relation), and there is no a priori reason for it to lie in the span of the irreducible characters that spans the trace space of H_n(q). The negative is guarded: conjugation by the CENTRE Δ²=(σ₁σ₂)³ returns exactly 1, the braid relation S(σ₁σ₂σ₁)=S(σ₂σ₁σ₂) holds, and every forced case (γ=β, γ trivial, σ₁-then-σ₁⁻¹ round trip) returns 1 — so the reducer is sound and the non-invariance is real, not a normal-form bug. Tested 4 base braids (w²=4₁, w³=Borromean, σ₁³ trefoil, σ₁σ₂) × 5 conjugators (σ₁, σ₂, σ₁⁻¹, σ₁σ₂, σ₂σ₁⁻¹), all exact in sympy. VERDICT: NOT conjugation-invariant (8/20 pairs differ) — the readout is a braid-conjugacy-dependent quantity, so it is NOT a knot invariant and cannot become one by any writhe normalisation. No Δ_λ, no c_k, nothing fitted, nothing wired.",
